    "original_title": "Soomaaliya oo xubin sare ku yeelatay Golaha Sare ee ururka caalamiga ah ee Tayada Daryeelka Caafimaadka",
    "slug": "somali-health-official-elected-to-board-of-global-healthcare-quality-organization",
    "summary": "Dr. Cabdirisaaq Yuusuf Axmed Jalaaludiin, Director of Somalia's National Health Insurance Agency, has been elected to the Board of ISQua, a global healthcare quality body, during its 42nd International Conference in Dublin.",
    "content": "Dr. Cabdirisaaq Yuusuf Axmed Jalaaludiin, the Director of Somalia's National Health Insurance Agency, has been elected as a full member of the Board of the International Society for Quality in Health Care (ISQua). The election took place during ISQua's 42nd International Conference, held in Dublin, Ireland. Dr. Jalaaludiin participated in the conference, where he presented on Somalia's progress in healthcare quality and insurance.\n\nISQua is a global organization dedicated to improving the quality and safety of healthcare worldwide. Its board sets strategic direction and promotes best practices among member countries. Dr. Jalaaludiin's election marks the first time a Somali official has held such a position, reflecting increased international recognition of Somalia's efforts to strengthen its health system.\n\nThe National Health Insurance Agency was established to provide affordable health coverage to Somali citizens. Under Dr. Jalaaludiin's leadership, the agency has worked to expand access to essential health services and improve care standards. His election to the ISQua board is seen as a validation of these efforts and a chance to learn from global peers.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development holds particular significance. Many diaspora members support relatives back home and rely on Somalia's health infrastructure when visiting or investing. Greater international integration of Somalia's health sector could lead to improved quality of care, making it easier for diaspora families to access reliable health services. It also opens avenues for diaspora health professionals to contribute expertise through partnerships with global bodies like ISQua.\n\nAdditionally, the election enhances Somalia's image abroad, potentially attracting more diaspora investment in healthcare and related sectors. Diaspora organizations often fund health projects and could benefit from the networks and standards promoted by ISQua. This recognition may also encourage diaspora-led initiatives to align with international quality benchmarks.\n\nDr. Jalaaludiin's new role is expected to facilitate knowledge exchange and capacity building for Somalia's health system. As a board member, he will participate in decision-making processes that influence global healthcare quality policies. This position could help Somalia access technical assistance and funding opportunities to further develop its health infrastructure.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/29/soomaaliya-oo-xubin-sare-ku-yeelatay-golaha-sare-ee-ururka-caalamiga-ah-ee-tayada-daryeelka-caafimaadka/",
